---
import ContentContainer from "../layouts/ContentContainer.astro";
---

<ContentContainer>
	<section class="border-b border-line">
		<ContentContainer>
			<a
				href="/services"
				class="font-mono text-xs tracking-[.14em] text-muted uppercase hover:text-ink"
				>← All services</a
			>
			<p class="mt-16 eyebrow">Custom Software</p>
			<h1
				class="mt-5 max-w-xl text-5xl font-semibold leading-[.96] tracking-[-.065em] sm:text-7xl"
			>
				Custom Software Development
			</h1>
			<p class="mt-8 max-w-2xl text-xl leading-8 text-muted">
				Purpose-built systems for the way your company actually works.
			</p>
		</ContentContainer>
	</section>
	<section
		class="mx-auto grid max-w-7xl gap-12 px-5 py-16 sm:px-8 sm:py-24 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_20rem] lg:gap-24 lg:px-12"
	>
		<div class="max-w-2xl text-lg text-muted rich-text">
			<h2 class="text-3xl mb-3">
				From operational needs to a clear product direction
			</h2>
			<p class="font-medium">
				Nightmare Software helps companies turn real operational challenges into
				software that supports the way their teams actually work. The process
				starts with discovery: understanding current workflows, systems,
				constraints, user roles, and the decisions the software needs to
				support. This may include reviewing existing tools, mapping handoffs,
				identifying repetitive work, and separating essential requirements from
				assumptions. The result is a practical foundation for deciding what to
				build and why.
			</p>
		</div>
		<div class="max-w-2xl text-lg text-muted rich-text">
			<h2 class="text-3xl mb-3">Architecture and product design</h2>
			<p class="font-medium">
				With the problem space defined, Nightmare Software shapes an
				architecture that can support the product's intended use, integrations,
				data flows, security needs, and future change. Product and interface
				design are grounded in the workflows uncovered during discovery, so
				screens, permissions, and system behavior reflect how users operate
				rather than forcing them into a generic process. Technical decisions are
				made with attention to maintainability, reliability, and the realities
				of the company's existing environment.
			</p>
		</div>
		<div class="max-w-2xl text-lg text-muted rich-text">
			<h2 class="text-3xl mb-3">Building software that can evolve</h2>
			<p class="font-medium">
				Development turns the agreed direction into working software through an
				iterative process. Nightmare Software can help establish the application
				structure, implement core capabilities, connect required services, and
				create the supporting data and deployment foundations. Work is organized
				so that assumptions can be reviewed as the product takes shape, allowing
				feedback from stakeholders and users to inform subsequent decisions
				instead of waiting until the end of a long, inflexible build.
			</p>
		</div>
		<div class="max-w-2xl text-lg text-muted rich-text">
			<h2 class="text-3xl mb-3">
				Ongoing improvement and technical stewardship
			</h2>
			<p class="font-medium">
				Custom software is rarely finished when the first useful version is
				released. Nightmare Software supports continued evolution through
				enhancements, workflow refinements, integrations, performance work,
				maintenance, and technical review. As the business changes, the software
				can be assessed against new requirements and adjusted without losing
				sight of the underlying architecture. This creates a practical
				partnership around the product's full lifecycle—from initial discovery
				and design through development, operation, and ongoing iteration.
			</p>
		</div>
		<aside class="h-fit border-t border-line pt-5 lg:sticky lg:top-8">
			<h2 class="font-mono text-xs tracking-[.14em] text-bright uppercase">
				Capabilities
			</h2>
			<ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-sm leading-6 text-muted">
				<li class="border-b border-line pb-3">
					Discovery & technical strategy
				</li>
				<li class="border-b border-line pb-3">Product design & architecture</li>
				<li class="border-b border-line pb-3">Web development</li>
				<li class="border-b border-line pb-3">Systems integration</li>
				<li class="border-b border-line pb-3">Ongoing optimization</li>
			</ul>
		</aside>
	</section>
</ContentContainer>
